     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I'm not questioning your authority to prescribe. The issue is that 6400 mg/day is twice the labeled maximum of 3200 mg/day, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. I know of no standard indication for that regimen. Ibuprofen's analgesic and anti-inflammatory effect plateaus well below it, so the main effect would be more risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that. Entering it would put a likely harmful order in front of nursing staff.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n\n- **Enter a dose within the maximum.** For example, 800 mg PO three times daily or 800 mg PO four times daily (3200 mg/day, the ceiling). Give me the dose and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Enter the order with a documented indication.** If there is a specific protocol or indication, such as a high-dose regimen guided by drug levels, tell me which one. I'd include it in the order note. You'd likely still need pharmacy verification and attending sign-off.\n- **Enter it yourself.** If you decide to proceed outside my recommendation, you can place it directly in the order system. It will probably trigger a pharmacy dose-range check, which is the safeguard meant to catch this.\n\nIf the goal is stronger pain control than a maximum-dose NSAID gives, I can help enter an adjunct instead, such as scheduled acetaminophen. Which would you like?"
      }
     ],
